Then, the upper bounds for the weighted numerical radius $w_t(T)$ and $w_t^2(T)$ in \eqref{c010} and \eqref{f1} are as shown in the Figure \ref{fig:01} and Figure \ref{fig:02} respectively.

Equations \eqref{c010} and \eqref{f1} provide parametric upper bounds for the numerical radii \( w(T) \) and \( w^2(T) \) respectively of a bounded linear operator \( T \) on a Hilbert space. These bounds are expressed in terms of the \( s \)-Aluthge transform with polar decomposition \( T = U|T| \). By varying the parameters \( s \in [0, 1] \) and \( t \in [0, 1] \), we visually represent how the bounds change together with these parameters. The resulting surface offers a clear visualization of the variation of the proposed upper bounds over the entire parameter region \( [0, 1]^2 \). 
Specifically, the plot allows us to identify the parameter ranges where the bounds are sharper, demonstrating the flexibility of the inequality concerning the choices of \( s \) and \( t \). This graphical representation complements the theoretical estimates by illustrating their numerical behavior for different combinations of parameters.








